        "id": 1000835
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-03-25T13:40:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It seems unfortunate that we need to support two different codepaths for parceling (and presumably for other things as well). Why is this needed? The commit message does not mention it.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 231,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 231,
        "endChar": 38
      },
      "revId": "55c70679818647f5ce04b6b17e403e4d374cd7ea",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b9542886_38750b5d",
        "filename": "framework/src/android/net/QosSocketInfo.java",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 231,
      "author": {
        "id": 1886292
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-03-29T03:28:21Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "QosSocketInfo is passed client-\u003eConnectivity \u0026 Connectivity-\u003eQoSProvider(Telephony).\n\nThe former case, we need to deliver PFD in QosSocketInfo because Connectivity checks socket\u0027s address \u0026 port with FD. However the latter case, QoSProvider doesn\u0027t need to use FD. we don\u0027t need to allow access of socket to Telephony.\nIf Connectivity passes PFD of UDP socket to telephony via binder transaction, We need to give a new SEPOLICY rule allowing read/write permission of udp_socket to telephony.\n  \u003e\u003e allow radio system_app:udp_socket { read write };\n\nI think this is not a good choice in point of security, so I intended not to pass PFD to QoSProvider.",
      "parentUuid": "3d8a4b8c_2ddb0fe5",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 231,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 231,
        "endChar": 38
      },
      "revId": "55c70679818647f5ce04b6b17e403e4d374cd7ea",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
